WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.400
- Okay, welcome.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.633
Thank you so much for joining
us for this special event.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.060
What a perfect day to spend
a rainy Friday afternoon

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.100
learning about Korean culture

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.623
while eating this delicious Korean food.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.463
As the sequel to the talk on Wednesday,

00:00:27.366 --> 00:00:32.070
Dr. Myung Chul, a Professor
Emeritus of History

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.810
at Dongguk University,

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:38.477
and currently a Professor
of Archeology at the,

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:45.290
I think, Samarkand State
University of Uzbekistan,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:49.650
will continue to shed light
on Korean history, culture,

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.390
art, and religion

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:55.983
in the greater East Asian
and Eurasian context.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.050
Again, in the interest of time,

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:06.050
and for the benefit of the
video recording of today's talk,

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:09.720
I'll ask you to write your questions

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.890
on the note being distributed.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.380
Do you guys have notes somewhere?

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.280
It'll be distributed,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.170
and I will collect them,

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.380
and then I'll read them after the talk.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.380
The format of today's
talk will be the same,

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.010
Professor Youn will be
giving a talk for 30 minutes,

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.440
which will be translated by my colleague,

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.970
Professor Juhn Ahn,

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:36.513
a Scholar of Korea Studies from
the University of Michigan.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.800
This event is sponsored by

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.670
the generous gift of Dr. Hwansoo Kim.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.810
And many thanks to council staff,

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.780
Kathy, and Injoon, and Dylan.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:52.950
Without further ado,

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.230
let's turn the floor over

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.303
to Professor Youn and Professor Ahn.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:00.893
- Hi, all.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.960
My name is Myung Youn Chul,

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.550
I'm a Korean, and historian,

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:09.630
and you see the program,
the video program.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.040
I'm a historian,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.960
and then I'm a poet.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.273
I wrote 14 poet books.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.392
Interesting?

00:02:18.392 --> 00:02:21.750
(students and Professor Youn laughing)

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.820
The day before yesterday, I have studied,

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.890
and with you,

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.920
and today is adopt new Tama,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.650
and I'm going to study.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.030
Okay?

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:38.030
And you know that I cannot
speak English well, so,

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:42.023
Professor Ahn.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:43.703
Okay?

00:02:43.703 --> 00:02:45.093
My study is a bit.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:47.543
Okay.

00:02:47.543 --> 00:02:48.514
- I'll try my best to translate.

00:02:48.514 --> 00:02:49.590
(Professor Youn laughing)

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:52.380
- And I have time, and
only 30 minutes here.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.640
30 minutes is very important
for me, and to Korean,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.990
and to America, and to the world.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:04.560
There's a reason, I think that,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.083
and now, okay?

00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:10.128
- Okay.

00:03:10.128 --> 00:03:14.461
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:16.800
So in the second meeting,

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.350
we'll be talking about
Korea's culture and thought,

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:20.514
or ideas.

00:03:20.514 --> 00:03:24.847
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.690
So as I explained last time on Wednesday,

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.160
Korea's culture and thought

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.713
is very different from
that of China and Japan.

00:03:36.513 --> 00:03:40.846
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.630
So as you can see here on this map,

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:55.180
the area of activity that
the Koreans encompassed

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.700
included not only the Korean
peninsula, but also Manchuria,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:01.680
so clearly there had
to be some connections

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.704
with the northern steppe
people and their cultures.

00:04:04.704 --> 00:04:08.910
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.510
Yeah, the large steppe region,

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.130
the prairies and the meadows up there,

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.340
that's the area that
exerted a lot of influence

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.531
on Manchuria, the early
Koreans, who occupied this area.

00:04:21.531 --> 00:04:25.864
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:30.780
Right, this is the culture of taigas,

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.790
these are the big forests

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:35.667
that occupy the regions in Siberia.

00:04:35.667 --> 00:04:38.310
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.883
And in the west there was,
of course, Chinese culture.

00:04:40.883 --> 00:04:45.216
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.110
And so in this eco-environment,

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.420
he believed that there's
a great difference

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:52.253
in the way people think

00:04:52.253 --> 00:04:54.226
and the way people lived, culturally.

00:04:54.226 --> 00:04:56.040
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.149
But there's something very important.

00:04:58.149 --> 00:05:02.482
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:07.920
So the Korean Peninsula,
obviously, is surrounded by water,

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.110
or the ocean, on three sides,

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:14.939
and so it clearly was
influenced by maritime culture.

00:05:14.939 --> 00:05:19.272
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.600
So this small territory that
we call the Korean Peninsula,

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.720
where the influences of
the northern steppes,

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.150
the forest cultures of the taigas,

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.900
the agricultural-agrarian
societies or cultures of China,

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.390
and the maritime culture
from Southeast Asia

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:46.983
and the oceans there,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.070
Korea's where they all meet,

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:53.227
and that's where Korean
civilization first arose.

00:05:53.227 --> 00:05:57.560
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:08.400
So, this may not be well known,

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.010
but Professor Youn believes that Korea

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.470
is the perfect eco-environment

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:17.230
for the reasons that he will
explain and has just explained.

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:21.563
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.560
So, naturally, in this
kind of environment,

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.134
Korea's culture cannot
but be diverse in nature.

00:06:29.134 --> 00:06:32.064
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:06:32.064 --> 00:06:33.089
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

00:06:33.089 --> 00:06:38.089
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:06:38.148 --> 00:06:38.981
(Professor Ahn laughing)

00:06:38.981 --> 00:06:41.520
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.620
Yeah, so it's a culture that's located

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:47.520
right in between two different
civilizations and cultures,

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:48.870
the one that developed in China,

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:52.530
and the other to the
other side of the strait.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:56.947
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.670
And in the Korean Peninsula,

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:01.830
in terms of the eco-environment,

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:05.280
you have the collection of
very different climates.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.860
So you have the colder climates

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.710
that are affiliated with
the northern steppe areas,

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:12.690
and then you also have the areas

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:14.160
that are more heavily affected

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.267
by the monsoons down in the south as well.

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:18.750
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:20.813
You're eating the kimchi that
you see here on the right now.

00:07:20.813 --> 00:07:21.828
(students laughing)

00:07:21.828 --> 00:07:22.661
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:07:22.661 --> 00:07:23.494
Is it good?

00:07:23.494 --> 00:07:25.898
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:07:25.898 --> 00:07:26.906
(students laughing)

00:07:26.906 --> 00:07:30.480
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:31.313
It's gotta be good,

00:07:31.313 --> 00:07:34.410
because kimchi is the
product, and the culmination,

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:36.787
of the mixing of all of
these different influences.

00:07:36.787 --> 00:07:40.740
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:42.060
And this can be explained

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:43.650
in anthropological terms as well,

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.624
but this is a fermentation culture.

00:07:45.624 --> 00:07:49.980
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:53.550
And another dish that is loved
by people all over the world

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:55.830
is the dish that was on the left,

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.200
that's made from fermented beans,

00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.198
that's doenjang.

00:07:59.198 --> 00:08:00.031
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:08:00.031 --> 00:08:01.650
And what you see, yeah, chongkukjang.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:02.640
It looks like everybody here (laughs)

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.200
knows what that is.
(students laughing)

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:05.434
It's nice and spicy.

00:08:05.434 --> 00:08:09.767
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:20.310
So, the food obviously is
the culmination product

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:23.610
of all these different
cultural and eco-influences,

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:25.760
and naturally, the
people who consume them,

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.635
their culture has to reflect
these influences as well.

00:08:30.635 --> 00:08:34.968
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:41.130
So this is the traditional
architecture of Korea.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:42.450
What you see here in this photo

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:45.480
are the traditional homes
that are still standing

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:46.770
in a place called Jeonju

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.407
in the southwestern regions of Korea.

00:08:49.407 --> 00:08:54.407
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.700
So, Korea has a very
unique housing system,

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.360
where they're furnished with
a heating system called Ondol,

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:02.670
where the heat is passed
through underneath the house

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:03.792
to heat the entire house.

00:09:03.792 --> 00:09:08.460
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:11.340
In the south, in addition
to the ondol system,

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:12.173
the heating system,

00:09:12.173 --> 00:09:13.500
there's also something called the maru,

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:16.446
and that's the area that
you see here in the photos.

00:09:16.446 --> 00:09:20.779
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.080
So this is the product of a wisdom

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.450
that is the combination of the cultures

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:28.830
of the warm regions of the south

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:30.661
and the cold areas of the north.

00:09:30.661 --> 00:09:34.994
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:09:40.354 --> 00:09:43.380
So, Korea has the unique
ability of creating a culture

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:45.870
through the combination and the clashes

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:46.970
of different cultures.

00:09:48.389 --> 00:09:53.389
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:56.948
Now, I'll talk about faith,
belief, and thought in Korea.

00:09:56.948 --> 00:10:01.281
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:07.320
There is a misconception
that in ancient Korea,

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:09.828
there was no unique
faith, belief, or thought.

00:10:09.828 --> 00:10:11.010
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:12.343
But that's not fact.

00:10:12.343 --> 00:10:16.830
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:18.660
Around the third or fourth century CE,

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:20.848
Buddhism comes into Korea.

00:10:20.848 --> 00:10:24.420
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:27.210
At the same time, Confucianism
arise in Korea as well,

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:29.819
but it did not exert
much influence in Korea.

00:10:29.819 --> 00:10:31.410
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:33.182
I'll ask you all a question.

00:10:33.182 --> 00:10:37.515
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:51.120
So there was a kingdom, or a
dynasty, known as proto-Joseon,

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:56.120
which lasted from 15th century
BCE to the 3rd century BCE,

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:59.070
but could a kingdom or dynasty like this

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:02.013
not have left written records or culture?

00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:07.263
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:19.320
I firmly believe that there
is a unique culture, right,

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:21.330
that that existed at the time,

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:23.700
faith and belief that existed at the time.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:25.980
And I believe that I
can find traces of this

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:28.440
in the archeological records,

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:31.337
and also in the customs of Koreans today.

00:11:31.337 --> 00:11:33.690
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:35.096
You know the word "shamanism," right?

00:11:35.096 --> 00:11:39.429
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:43.260
The word shaman is not obviously English,

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:45.114
but a word that comes from Manchuria.

00:11:45.114 --> 00:11:49.447
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:53.760
So we can say that shamanism

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:55.620
is a very unique religion or faith

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:57.030
that emerged in the regions

00:11:57.030 --> 00:12:00.576
that encompasses Manchuria
and the Korean Peninsula.

00:12:00.576 --> 00:12:04.909
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:09.240
Of course, there is traces of shamanism

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:10.500
in the Baikal Lake area,

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:12.348
in the premier mountain ranges,

00:12:12.348 --> 00:12:15.665
in that area in Central Asia as well.

00:12:15.665 --> 00:12:20.497
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:12:20.497 --> 00:12:22.123
I'd like to say a lot of things,

00:12:22.123 --> 00:12:23.820
I have a lot to say about the subject,

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:25.743
but I obviously won't have the time.

00:12:25.743 --> 00:12:30.076
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:35.817
If you wanna know more, please
invite me out again. (laughs)

00:12:35.817 --> 00:12:37.336
(students laughing)

00:12:37.336 --> 00:12:41.669
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:45.750
The reason why we cannot
but have a culture like this

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.763
is because of the eco-environment.

00:12:47.763 --> 00:12:52.170
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:53.460
In every place I went,

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:54.900
there were mountains, there were streams,

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:56.464
and there were large plains.

00:12:56.464 --> 00:12:58.470
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:00.307
But they're all very different.

00:13:00.307 --> 00:13:04.640
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:10.260
So, one of the unique characteristics

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:12.840
of the mountains, and the
streams, and the plains in Korea

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:14.340
is that they are friendly,

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:16.710
and that's what makes them very,

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:17.880
friendly to human beings,

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:20.130
and this is what makes the
eco-environment of Korea

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:21.186
very unique.

00:13:21.186 --> 00:13:25.519
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:29.070
That's why Koreans are so kind,

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:31.974
and there are so many kind
Koreans in this room right now.

00:13:31.974 --> 00:13:35.700
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.439
And this cannot but be an
old historical tradition.

00:13:38.439 --> 00:13:43.439
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:13:43.465 --> 00:13:44.820
And in a natural environment like this,

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:48.251
you cannot but develop a very
strong sense of community.

00:13:48.251 --> 00:13:51.900
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:52.733
And therefore,

00:13:52.733 --> 00:13:54.497
Koreans think of themselves
as one big family.

00:13:54.497 --> 00:13:55.698
(page turning)

00:13:55.698 --> 00:14:00.031
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:07.770
And I expect that this
kind of belief and thought

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:10.504
will exert greater influence
in the world as well.

00:14:10.504 --> 00:14:14.837
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:25.680
As a historian, I not only
worked on historical facts,

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:27.306
but also thought and faith,

00:14:27.306 --> 00:14:32.306
and I've also worked on other
world civilizations as well.

00:14:32.565 --> 00:14:36.898
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.010
And this is because history exists

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.260
not only to uncover lost facts,

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:47.010
but because for the sake of
world civilization as a whole,

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:48.376
and its future.

00:14:48.376 --> 00:14:52.709
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.190
For me, my family's important,

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:05.310
Koreans are important,
everybody here is important.

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:06.397
Everybody in the world is important.

00:15:06.397 --> 00:15:08.320
(page turning)

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:12.653
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:16.650
And so I proffer today a new thought,

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:20.010
and that is the notion
of non-civilization.

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:20.843
- Non-civilization.

00:15:20.843 --> 00:15:23.370
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:24.684
- Please remember the
word "non-civilization."

00:15:24.684 --> 00:15:26.799
(students laughing)

00:15:26.799 --> 00:15:28.560
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.413
And this is a new theory by Dr. Youn.

00:15:33.481 --> 00:15:36.120
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:37.911
Now let's look at the culture of Korea.

00:15:37.911 --> 00:15:40.980
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:42.660
A lot of people have misunderstandings

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:46.800
about Korean culture and
beauty, or aesthetics.

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:47.809
- Okay, I'm sorry.

00:15:47.809 --> 00:15:52.142
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:57.210
- As I explained last time,

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.790
the culture and thought of Korea

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:02.970
has been misconceived and misrepresented

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.945
by Japan and Western scholars.

00:16:05.945 --> 00:16:09.960
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:14.910
So Koreans are said to have a lot of Han,

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:16.350
resentment or sorrow,

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:18.630
because it has been subjected

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:23.148
to frequent incursions and
invasions by other people.

00:16:23.148 --> 00:16:24.870
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:27.243
Do you like do you like BTS? (laughs)

00:16:27.243 --> 00:16:28.331
- Yes? Okay.

00:16:28.331 --> 00:16:32.720
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:36.390
- Look at their dance,

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:39.724
how active, and how
much movement there is.

00:16:39.724 --> 00:16:44.057
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:49.740
But despite the fact that there is

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:51.840
this very active component
to Korean culture,

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:54.030
we've been taught, misleadingly,

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:58.894
that we are very quiet and
passive, right, and secluded.

00:16:58.894 --> 00:17:01.350
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:02.220
But as a historian,

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:04.717
I can tell you that that is
not actually based in fact.

00:17:04.717 --> 00:17:06.960
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:10.380
And Koreans are often described as people

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:11.550
who practice toadyism,

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:13.243
or serving the great.

00:17:13.243 --> 00:17:17.576
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:17:20.610 --> 00:17:22.980
The only people in that region

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:24.630
that has continued to see themselves

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:29.040
and maintain their
identities for 3,500 years

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:29.955
are Koreans.

00:17:29.955 --> 00:17:31.470
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:33.030
And therefore we have a very strong sense

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:34.111
of self-consciousness.

00:17:34.111 --> 00:17:37.860
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:41.880
So I refer to the characteristic
of the Korean people

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:43.130
as free-will.

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:47.463
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:53.940
So Korea is misunderstood often
as the land of morning calm,

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:56.523
but I see it as the land of very active.

00:17:57.784 --> 00:18:01.650
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:02.887
Have you ever seen this word before,

00:18:02.887 --> 00:18:04.293
"Mo-stability" culture?

00:18:05.481 --> 00:18:06.780
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:08.152
- Okay, impossible.

00:18:08.152 --> 00:18:09.703
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:18:09.703 --> 00:18:11.100
(students laughing)

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:13.380
- That's obvious that you
haven't seen this before,

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:15.531
because I coined the term.

00:18:15.531 --> 00:18:18.630
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:21.333
In the past, Korea's
culture has been described

00:18:21.333 --> 00:18:24.528
almost exclusively in terms of stability.

00:18:24.528 --> 00:18:29.528
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:18:29.625 --> 00:18:32.400
In our last meeting,

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:34.110
one of your professors, Professor Westoff,

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:36.840
referred to Korea as a stability culture.

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:39.000
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:40.410
But the culture he's referring

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.150
to is the culture of the Joseon period,

00:18:42.150 --> 00:18:43.863
which only lasted 500 years.

00:18:43.863 --> 00:18:45.240
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:46.680
But above that, or before that,

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:49.072
there's 3,000 years of Korean history.

00:18:49.072 --> 00:18:53.405
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:57.570
If you really wanna understand Korea,

00:18:57.570 --> 00:18:58.890
the genetic Korea,

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:02.250
you need to go beyond the
500 years of Joseon history

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:03.930
into a steep past.

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:08.263
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:14.280
So that's why I strongly believe

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:17.280
that Korean culture is not
just a culture of stability,

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.470
but a culture of mobility and stability,

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:23.100
and that's why I coined
the term mo-stability.

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:23.933
- Okay.

00:19:23.933 --> 00:19:24.766
Good.

00:19:24.766 --> 00:19:26.400
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:28.783
- So I would love for you
all to remember this word.

00:19:28.783 --> 00:19:31.380
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:34.920
And the most representative
kingdom of this kind of culture

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:37.953
is the ancient Korean Kingdom of Koguryo.

00:19:38.831 --> 00:19:40.340
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:44.381
And Koguryo is what I studied for my PhD.

00:19:44.381 --> 00:19:47.539
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:19:47.539 --> 00:19:49.268
(students laughing)

00:19:49.268 --> 00:19:50.363
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

00:19:50.363 --> 00:19:51.840
(students and Professor Youn laughing)

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:52.740
You may not know,

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:55.542
but I'm actually a very
famous person in Korea,

00:19:55.542 --> 00:19:59.970
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:19:59.970 --> 00:20:01.140
and that's why I was invited

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:04.023
to the greatest university
in the world, Yale.

00:20:04.023 --> 00:20:08.356
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:13.230
And he hopes that whatever
he shares with you here,

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:14.610
at this great institution,

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:16.382
will then spread out to
the rest of the world.

00:20:16.382 --> 00:20:18.777
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:20:18.777 --> 00:20:21.180
And that's why I changed,

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:22.980
I went from one flight to the next,

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:25.379
and made this long journey to Yale.

00:20:25.379 --> 00:20:29.712
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:37.020
So, Koguryo culture and
history has deep connections

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:39.504
with Central-Asian history and culture.

00:20:39.504 --> 00:20:43.921
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:50.998
The dance and the music
that are referenced

00:20:50.998 --> 00:20:55.998
in the stories about Koguryo
in the ancient sources,

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:58.360
they clearly have a
connection to Central-Asia.

00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:04.530
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:21:04.544 --> 00:21:05.640
Here in America,

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:09.420
there is a tendency to
lift Japan up on a pedestal

00:21:09.420 --> 00:21:11.115
and to idolize it.

00:21:11.115 --> 00:21:12.810
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:15.719
I do think that it's worthy of
that kind of respect as well.

00:21:15.719 --> 00:21:20.052
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:28.530
But it's a historical fact
that in ancient times,

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:30.966
it was the people who lived
in the Korean Peninsula

00:21:30.966 --> 00:21:34.920
who crossed over the strait
into the Japanese archipelago,

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:37.162
who established the earliest civilization.

00:21:37.162 --> 00:21:41.495
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:21:45.042 --> 00:21:46.590
And this is widely known,

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:50.100
because we have evidence in
the archeological remains,

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:53.202
and also in the foundation myths.

00:21:53.202 --> 00:21:56.422
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:21:56.422 --> 00:21:58.053
From now on, I'll show you some pictures.

00:21:58.053 --> 00:22:00.990
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:04.233
So these are parts of a
golden crown used in Koguryo.

00:22:05.316 --> 00:22:07.380
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:10.254
There must have been a lot,
but we only have a few left.

00:22:10.254 --> 00:22:14.587
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:22:17.749 --> 00:22:19.335
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

00:22:19.335 --> 00:22:21.900
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:24.450
So, this is the capital of Koguryo,

00:22:24.450 --> 00:22:26.910
but the first and the
second capital of Koguryo

00:22:26.910 --> 00:22:28.170
are in Manchuria.

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:29.003
- [Professor Youn] Yeah.

00:22:29.003 --> 00:22:33.298
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:39.570
- [Professor Ahn] So this
is a picture of the area

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:42.060
behind the second capital, Gungnae-seong,

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:44.319
and there are a lot of
tombs, as you can see.

00:22:44.319 --> 00:22:47.970
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:51.330
So, these capitals had this
very impressive structure

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:52.163
inland.

00:22:52.163 --> 00:22:55.007
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:22:55.007 --> 00:22:56.846
I have something to say about this.

00:22:56.846 --> 00:23:01.290
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:03.570
The European, Japanese,
and Chinese castles

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:05.297
are very big and beautiful.

00:23:05.297 --> 00:23:10.297
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:12.240
So Korean castles are big,

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:15.154
but they're not aesthetically pleasing.

00:23:15.154 --> 00:23:15.987
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:23:15.987 --> 00:23:16.820
There's a reason.

00:23:17.891 --> 00:23:22.224
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:23:24.026 --> 00:23:25.590
The castles that were built in Korea

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:28.260
were meant to protect the
people and the commoners,

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:30.049
and not necessarily the elites.

00:23:30.049 --> 00:23:34.382
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:47.530
He thinks that the Koguryo
castles are so much more valuable

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:49.470
in comparison to, say,

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:53.190
the Greek polis, or the
grand European castles,

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:54.990
precisely because these were castles

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:57.641
that were built to protect
the commoners, or the people.

00:23:57.641 --> 00:24:00.330
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:04.030
Shouldn't we consider this to
be the origins of democracy?

00:24:04.030 --> 00:24:07.920
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:10.530
I hope you can all start
to develop new ideas

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:12.360
and new perceptions of Korea

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:15.394
with this as the occasion.

00:24:15.394 --> 00:24:20.353
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:24:20.353 --> 00:24:21.780
So, in this area,

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:24.450
there are about 12,000 of these tombs

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:28.073
that were excavated and
studied by scholars.

00:24:28.073 --> 00:24:32.406
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:35.460
So there are about 12 of these pyramids.

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:36.930
The pyramid that you see here

00:24:36.930 --> 00:24:38.918
is the one that is the most complete.

00:24:38.918 --> 00:24:40.320
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:42.262
I'll offer a very simple
explanation of it.

00:24:42.262 --> 00:24:45.180
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:48.750
So, according to the
Koguryo measurement system,

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:50.731
this is a hundred chon.

00:24:50.731 --> 00:24:51.960
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:56.370
And it's a right-triangle. (laughs)

00:24:56.370 --> 00:24:57.615
- Okay.

00:24:57.615 --> 00:25:00.960
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:03.233
- It consists of seven floors.

00:25:03.233 --> 00:25:05.520
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:09.180
Each floor consists of three steps.

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:10.102
- Okay.

00:25:10.102 --> 00:25:11.250
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:13.568
- And that makes it a total of 21 steps.

00:25:13.568 --> 00:25:15.570
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:16.830
And this is the number

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:19.893
that Koreans love the most, 21. (laughs)

00:25:19.893 --> 00:25:24.390
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:25:24.390 --> 00:25:26.250
In Korea, when a child is born,

00:25:26.250 --> 00:25:28.140
they don't just see that
as the birth of a child,

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:29.600
but the birth of a universe.

00:25:29.600 --> 00:25:30.870
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:32.665
So a lot of rituals are performed.

00:25:32.665 --> 00:25:36.998
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:42.960
And one of those rituals
is to bind this thread

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:45.780
and create a boundary around the child

00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:48.387
into which others are
not permitted to enter.

00:25:48.387 --> 00:25:49.410
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:50.982
For 21 days.

00:25:50.982 --> 00:25:51.815
- Okay.

00:25:51.815 --> 00:25:56.580
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:25:56.580 --> 00:25:58.620
- For Koreans, the number
three, and the number seven

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:00.421
are very, very important numbers.

00:26:00.421 --> 00:26:04.754
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:08.070
And so in the foundation myths,

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:11.042
we have structures like
this that are still extant.

00:26:11.042 --> 00:26:12.240
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:13.073
I'll go on,

00:26:13.073 --> 00:26:14.413
I'll move on.

00:26:14.413 --> 00:26:19.260
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:21.090
This is from the 4th century,

00:26:21.090 --> 00:26:23.157
and it's a mural from a Koguryo tomb.

00:26:23.157 --> 00:26:27.490
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:29.910
And as you can see here,

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:34.910
there is an army, or a standing
army, that consists of men

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:40.048
outfitted with heavy military technology.

00:26:40.048 --> 00:26:42.690
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:47.355
Here, 259 of these mountain
soldiers are depicted.

00:26:47.355 --> 00:26:51.360
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:53.100
And these are all mounted soldiers

00:26:53.100 --> 00:26:58.100
that are wearing armor
that was made out of iron.

00:26:59.719 --> 00:27:02.880
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:06.298
This one is in Pyungyang, the
other one is in Manchuria.

00:27:06.298 --> 00:27:10.260
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:27:10.260 --> 00:27:12.607
And what you have here are,

00:27:12.607 --> 00:27:14.211
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

00:27:14.211 --> 00:27:15.090
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:27:15.090 --> 00:27:17.160
so, these are golden arrow tips,

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:18.810
and that is a stirrup,

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:21.210
where you put your feet
when you mount a horse,

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:22.410
that's made out of gold.

00:27:23.289 --> 00:27:26.429
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:27:26.429 --> 00:27:27.262
On the left,

00:27:27.262 --> 00:27:30.346
what you see is the
representation of a Koguryo woman.

00:27:30.346 --> 00:27:32.760
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:34.140
I know the face is pretty,

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:35.913
but I'd like you to focus on the clothing.

00:27:35.913 --> 00:27:38.280
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:39.113
And as you can see,

00:27:39.113 --> 00:27:41.120
the clothing is very
different from the Hanbok,

00:27:41.120 --> 00:27:43.110
or the traditional Korean clothing,

00:27:43.110 --> 00:27:44.795
that was worn during the Joseon Period.

00:27:44.795 --> 00:27:49.350
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:52.890
And what you see here is
basically an image of somebody

00:27:52.890 --> 00:27:54.840
who would be better-suited

00:27:54.840 --> 00:27:57.982
for mounting a horse and fighting.

00:27:57.982 --> 00:28:02.315
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:28:05.430 --> 00:28:07.650
These are arrows that were made

00:28:07.650 --> 00:28:09.720
a thousand years before the Mongol army

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:13.208
was outfitted with these bows and arrows.

00:28:13.208 --> 00:28:14.790
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:28:14.790 --> 00:28:16.689
These are all from the Koguryo.

00:28:16.689 --> 00:28:17.910
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:28:17.910 --> 00:28:19.459
They're very beautiful
murals, found inside --

00:28:19.459 --> 00:28:21.388
- Yes, very beautiful.

00:28:21.388 --> 00:28:25.020
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:28:25.020 --> 00:28:26.610
- I've had the great opportunity

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:28.260
to actually enter these tombs myself

00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:30.452
and personally gaze upon these murals.

00:28:30.452 --> 00:28:33.360
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:34.650
And of course,

00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:39.030
this is all observations that were made

00:28:39.030 --> 00:28:40.770
in areas that aren't Korea today,

00:28:40.770 --> 00:28:42.520
but in areas outside of.

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:44.718
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:28:44.718 --> 00:28:45.551
I just recalled something right now.

00:28:45.551 --> 00:28:48.006
(Professor Youn laughing)

00:28:48.006 --> 00:28:50.460
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:52.007
In 1995,

00:28:52.007 --> 00:28:56.430
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:28:56.430 --> 00:28:59.610
I purchased a horse in North Manchuria,

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:03.063
and I rode it all the way
down to South Manchuria.

00:29:03.063 --> 00:29:04.800
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:07.684
And it's because I'm a
real historian. (laughs)

00:29:07.684 --> 00:29:09.560
(students laughing)

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:13.350
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:14.700
Because in Korea,

00:29:14.700 --> 00:29:18.257
Koreans have forgotten
the horse-riding culture.

00:29:18.257 --> 00:29:20.220
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:23.259
And the strong mentality,
they've lost that as well.

00:29:23.259 --> 00:29:24.720
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:26.700
And the strong sense of free-will,

00:29:26.700 --> 00:29:27.756
they've lost that.

00:29:27.756 --> 00:29:31.410
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:35.520
And to experience that for
myself, I purchased the horse,

00:29:35.520 --> 00:29:38.739
and made the trek to South Manchuria.

00:29:38.739 --> 00:29:43.072
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:29:50.070 --> 00:29:51.660
And all of this was documented

00:29:51.660 --> 00:29:54.180
in documentaries, and books, and whatnot.

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:57.030
And some of these, the books,

00:29:57.030 --> 00:29:59.431
they'll be donated here, to Yale.

00:29:59.431 --> 00:30:03.450
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:30:03.450 --> 00:30:05.700
And for the students who can read Korean,

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:08.354
he highly recommends that
you take a look at his books.

00:30:08.354 --> 00:30:09.840
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:12.327
They're really fun. (laughs)

00:30:12.327 --> 00:30:17.312
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:30:17.312 --> 00:30:18.827
On the left, what you see is a bird

00:30:18.827 --> 00:30:21.360
that you can pretty much consider

00:30:21.360 --> 00:30:22.815
to be something like a phoenix.

00:30:22.815 --> 00:30:27.148
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:30.270
When you see this bird,

00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:33.780
do you feel that what you're
witnessing is something static,

00:30:33.780 --> 00:30:37.457
or something that is
active, mobile, and moving.

00:30:37.457 --> 00:30:41.220
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:30:41.220 --> 00:30:43.650
And if you look at his
head, it's tilted up,

00:30:43.650 --> 00:30:46.668
and it's gazing far into the distance.

00:30:46.668 --> 00:30:47.501
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:30:47.501 --> 00:30:48.450
And look at its feet.

00:30:49.509 --> 00:30:54.336
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:30:54.336 --> 00:30:57.336
The legs and the feet are very tiny,

00:30:59.460 --> 00:31:02.551
but the talons dig deep into reality.

00:31:02.551 --> 00:31:06.884
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:15.990
He believes that Gura folk

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:17.700
were able to draw things like this

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:20.370
because they had one foot
strongly planted in reality,

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:22.200
but they were always looking

00:31:22.200 --> 00:31:25.489
in the honor for, and
aspiring for greater things.

00:31:25.489 --> 00:31:27.810
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:31:27.810 --> 00:31:31.153
I'd love to say more, but
the problem is always time.

00:31:31.153 --> 00:31:32.970
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:34.020
These are golden crowns

00:31:34.020 --> 00:31:35.469
from the ancient Korean kingdom of Baekje.

00:31:35.469 --> 00:31:36.390
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:31:36.390 --> 00:31:38.335
They're very beautiful.

00:31:38.335 --> 00:31:40.380
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:31:40.380 --> 00:31:43.313
Also golden crowns from Baekje.

00:31:43.313 --> 00:31:45.720
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:47.940
And these are bronze crowns, I'm sorry,

00:31:47.940 --> 00:31:50.173
gilt-bronze crowns from Baekje.

00:31:50.173 --> 00:31:51.300
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:52.203
And shoes.

00:31:55.239 --> 00:31:56.730
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:31:56.730 --> 00:32:01.650
And more crowns from another
ancient Korean federation,

00:32:01.650 --> 00:32:03.003
known as Gaya.

00:32:03.003 --> 00:32:04.781
(Professor Youn and
Ahn speaking in Korean)

00:32:04.781 --> 00:32:09.114
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:12.480
I'll offer a brief commentary

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:15.718
on the connections
between Eurasia and Korea.

00:32:15.718 --> 00:32:20.051
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:32:21.307 --> 00:32:24.011
As I mentioned briefly last time,

00:32:24.011 --> 00:32:27.820
the Asian continent had been
connected for over 5,000 years

00:32:29.340 --> 00:32:33.035
in terms of the west and the
east ends of the continent.

00:32:33.035 --> 00:32:35.795
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:32:35.795 --> 00:32:37.607
Oh, I'm sorry, not 5,000, 50,000.

00:32:37.607 --> 00:32:39.690
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:41.400
The road that he just pointed out,

00:32:41.400 --> 00:32:42.300
that's the Silk Road,

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:43.650
but that's not the only one,

00:32:43.650 --> 00:32:44.610
there are a lot of routes

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:45.810
through which this interaction

00:32:45.810 --> 00:32:47.919
between the east and west took place.

00:32:47.919 --> 00:32:52.252
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:55.754
So as you can see,

00:32:55.754 --> 00:32:58.260
all the routes that interpenetrate

00:32:58.260 --> 00:32:59.580
through the Asian continent,

00:32:59.580 --> 00:33:01.200
they all culminate in one point,

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:02.787
and that of course is
the Korean Peninsula.

00:33:02.787 --> 00:33:06.690
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:10.813
Naturally, Korea has deep
connections with Central Asia.

00:33:10.813 --> 00:33:13.620
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:16.910
The evidence of this connection
you can find in language.

00:33:16.910 --> 00:33:18.720
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:33:18.720 --> 00:33:21.145
And we call this the
Altaic language connection.

00:33:21.145 --> 00:33:23.790
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:33:23.790 --> 00:33:27.780
Some linguists do deny the
Altaic language theory.

00:33:27.780 --> 00:33:30.540
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:32.970
But it is very clear
that there were people

00:33:32.970 --> 00:33:34.459
who used the Altaic languages.

00:33:34.459 --> 00:33:36.150
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:33:36.150 --> 00:33:38.034
Do you know who Genghis Khan is?

00:33:38.034 --> 00:33:38.867
- Chingiz Kan.

00:33:38.867 --> 00:33:39.700
- Or it's Chingiz Khan.

00:33:39.700 --> 00:33:40.533
- Chingiz Khan, Chingiz Han,

00:33:40.533 --> 00:33:41.896
Chingiz K-han.

00:33:41.896 --> 00:33:46.530
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:47.910
- In the Altaic language,

00:33:47.910 --> 00:33:50.751
rulers or leaders refer
to themselves as Han.

00:33:50.751 --> 00:33:53.070
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:56.370
And the names of kingdom or countries,

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:59.350
you often find the word
in the names as well.

00:33:59.350 --> 00:34:03.683
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:09.390
We find references to this
as early as the 5th century.

00:34:09.390 --> 00:34:10.950
But since the time of Chingiz Khan,

00:34:10.950 --> 00:34:12.600
you see even more people

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:15.995
referring themselves in
high possessions as Han.

00:34:15.995 --> 00:34:20.856
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:34:20.856 --> 00:34:22.290
In my research,

00:34:22.290 --> 00:34:27.290
I discovered over 39 places
that refer to themselves as Han.

00:34:27.817 --> 00:34:30.210
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:34:30.210 --> 00:34:33.450
And today, Korea is also
called the Han country.

00:34:33.450 --> 00:34:34.623
- Yes, Han connective.

00:34:36.140 --> 00:34:38.700
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:34:38.700 --> 00:34:42.013
- They refer to the sky,
or the heaven, as Tengri.

00:34:42.013 --> 00:34:46.346
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:51.480
In the Turkish cultural sphere,

00:34:51.480 --> 00:34:55.192
they refer to the sky, and
the god of the sky, as Tengri.

00:34:55.192 --> 00:34:58.530
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:34:58.530 --> 00:34:59.940
I'll skip all the rest,

00:34:59.940 --> 00:35:02.044
but I'll focus on just one thing now.

00:35:02.044 --> 00:35:06.840
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:35:06.840 --> 00:35:08.280
Where's the one place in Eurasia

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:12.090
where a very, very long and protracted war

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:13.710
is taking place right now?

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:14.580
Do you know?

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:15.423
In Central Asia?

00:35:16.394 --> 00:35:17.400
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:18.770
A very, very --

00:35:19.830 --> 00:35:20.663
- Ukraine.

00:35:20.663 --> 00:35:22.519
- Yeah, right, it's Ukraine.

00:35:22.519 --> 00:35:25.672
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:35:25.672 --> 00:35:29.510
The big sea below Ukraine
is called the Black Sea.

00:35:29.510 --> 00:35:32.070
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:35:32.070 --> 00:35:34.811
The Black Sea is a very, very harsh sea.

00:35:34.811 --> 00:35:39.144
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:44.370
A long time ago, I took a boat or a ship,

00:35:44.370 --> 00:35:45.450
not sure which,

00:35:45.450 --> 00:35:47.250
all the way from Korea
to the Mediterranean

00:35:47.250 --> 00:35:48.384
into the Black Sea

00:35:48.384 --> 00:35:49.440
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:35:49.440 --> 00:35:50.666
for 90 days.

00:35:50.666 --> 00:35:54.999
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:35:57.450 --> 00:35:58.920
It was a big ship that he took,

00:35:58.920 --> 00:36:00.330
but when he entered the Black Sea,

00:36:00.330 --> 00:36:03.258
it was so rough that he couldn't
really navigate it there.

00:36:03.258 --> 00:36:05.610
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:36:05.610 --> 00:36:07.758
And the sea there was actually black.

00:36:07.758 --> 00:36:09.300
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:36:09.300 --> 00:36:11.031
And that's why it's called the Black Sea.

00:36:11.031 --> 00:36:13.410
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:36:13.410 --> 00:36:15.450
But the Turkish referred
to it as the Gara denes.

00:36:15.450 --> 00:36:16.344
- Yes.

00:36:16.344 --> 00:36:17.177
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:36:17.177 --> 00:36:18.150
- Kara means black.

00:36:18.150 --> 00:36:19.128
- In Buddhism.

00:36:19.128 --> 00:36:20.640
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:22.817
- In Japan, black is kuro.

00:36:22.817 --> 00:36:27.150
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:33.443
And as you can see,

00:36:33.443 --> 00:36:35.160
there's linguistic
evidence of the connection

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:38.040
between Central Asia all the
way over to China, to Korea,

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:39.269
and of course, Japan.

00:36:39.269 --> 00:36:43.602
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:52.200
In Samarkand, where I am right now,

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:54.000
you find this mural from the 7th century,

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:54.833
and in the mural,

00:36:54.833 --> 00:36:56.400
what you see are actually representations

00:36:56.400 --> 00:36:58.789
of two envoys from Koguryo.

00:36:58.789 --> 00:37:03.122
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:11.300
In the midst of the
protracted war with China

00:37:11.300 --> 00:37:12.960
that lasted 70 years,

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:15.000
Gora actually sent envoys

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:18.810
4,000 miles across the Asian continent

00:37:18.810 --> 00:37:21.091
to remote locations like Samarkand.

00:37:21.091 --> 00:37:22.350
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:37:22.350 --> 00:37:24.358
And this is a very remarkable fact.

00:37:24.358 --> 00:37:27.810
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:37:27.810 --> 00:37:31.410
And this is located right
next to Samarkand University,

00:37:31.410 --> 00:37:32.727
where he's working right now.

00:37:32.727 --> 00:37:36.929
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:37:36.929 --> 00:37:37.762
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

00:37:37.762 --> 00:37:38.595
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:37:38.595 --> 00:37:39.428
Oh.
(students laughing)

00:37:39.428 --> 00:37:40.830
A lot of people, (laughs)

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:42.720
a lot of people argue that my face

00:37:42.720 --> 00:37:44.388
is the face of Koguryo.

00:37:44.388 --> 00:37:46.290
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:48.138
This is not a joke,
it's for real. (laughs)

00:37:48.138 --> 00:37:52.471
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:37:54.510 --> 00:37:58.230
In a place called Kuri,
located not far from Seoul,

00:37:58.230 --> 00:38:00.720
there is a bronze statue,

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:03.628
and the model for that
statue is our Professor Youn.

00:38:03.628 --> 00:38:04.923
(students laughing)

00:38:04.923 --> 00:38:09.900
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:38:09.900 --> 00:38:12.911
I'm a historian, so I
base everything on fact.

00:38:12.911 --> 00:38:17.244
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:38:18.480 --> 00:38:20.128
We're almost done.

00:38:20.128 --> 00:38:23.610
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:38:23.610 --> 00:38:26.636
So let's talk about religion,
and religious faith.

00:38:26.636 --> 00:38:31.636
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:38:31.740 --> 00:38:33.480
In Eurasia or Central Asia,

00:38:33.480 --> 00:38:37.059
they worship the sky,
or heaven, or tengri.

00:38:37.059 --> 00:38:41.392
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:38:43.140 --> 00:38:44.730
And in the ancient kingdoms of Korea,

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:47.430
the rulers also refer to themselves

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:48.910
as the sons of the sky.

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:52.080
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:38:52.080 --> 00:38:54.450
And this will cross the
ocean, or the strait,

00:38:54.450 --> 00:38:56.580
over into Japan, and the
same thing happens there.

00:38:56.580 --> 00:39:00.870
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:39:00.870 --> 00:39:03.180
And there are of course
remarkable similarities

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:05.760
between the foundation
myths of Japan and Korea

00:39:05.760 --> 00:39:06.814
as a consequence.

00:39:06.814 --> 00:39:09.090
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:39:09.090 --> 00:39:11.597
If you look at the Japanese
flag there, it's the sun.

00:39:11.597 --> 00:39:14.310
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:39:14.310 --> 00:39:15.690
And obviously, in Korea,

00:39:15.690 --> 00:39:17.936
we worshiped the sun in
ancient times as well.

00:39:17.936 --> 00:39:22.269
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:39:23.890 --> 00:39:25.950
All you need to do is recall

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:28.410
the image of the raft that I took,

00:39:28.410 --> 00:39:31.545
that I showed you all in my last lecture.

00:39:31.545 --> 00:39:35.878
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:39:45.420 --> 00:39:48.870
So these elements, the cosmic
mountain, and the cosmic tree,

00:39:48.870 --> 00:39:50.070
these are things that Korea

00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:51.565
has in common with Central Asia.

00:39:51.565 --> 00:39:55.898
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:39:57.090 --> 00:39:59.880
A unique bronze culture
develops in Manchuria

00:39:59.880 --> 00:40:01.129
and the Korean Peninsula.

00:40:01.129 --> 00:40:05.462
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:10.650
Around the 12th century BCE,

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:15.650
the bronze culture of the
steppes enters into Korea.

00:40:16.255 --> 00:40:20.588
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:40:21.870 --> 00:40:24.270
People in this culture

00:40:24.270 --> 00:40:26.250
exerts a heavy influence on their growth

00:40:26.250 --> 00:40:27.199
of Korean antiquity.

00:40:27.199 --> 00:40:28.032
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:40:28.032 --> 00:40:29.846
And I'll show you evidence of this now.

00:40:29.846 --> 00:40:34.179
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:42.660
From the 5th, 6th century CE,

00:40:42.660 --> 00:40:44.490
all the way down to the 12th century CE,

00:40:44.490 --> 00:40:49.490
what you see are these glass
ornaments and glassware,

00:40:52.140 --> 00:40:56.670
and these are all imported
items from Persia,

00:40:56.670 --> 00:40:58.583
or the Middle East.

00:40:58.583 --> 00:41:00.000
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:01.581
These are golden crowns.

00:41:01.581 --> 00:41:05.914
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:10.380
The kingdom that has the
most number of golden crowns

00:41:10.380 --> 00:41:12.595
is the ancient Korean kingdom of Silla.

00:41:12.595 --> 00:41:15.450
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:18.480
And the connection between
Silla and Central Asia

00:41:18.480 --> 00:41:19.511
is very, very deep.

00:41:19.511 --> 00:41:21.210
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:41:21.210 --> 00:41:23.465
You all know Excalibur, right?

00:41:23.465 --> 00:41:25.312
The sword?

00:41:25.312 --> 00:41:29.645
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:41:30.810 --> 00:41:32.733
A beautiful blade,

00:41:34.289 --> 00:41:36.870
like the one that you
see here on the slide.

00:41:36.870 --> 00:41:40.028
This was discovered in the
ancient Korean kingdom of Silla.

00:41:40.028 --> 00:41:44.779
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:41:44.779 --> 00:41:46.470
In Korea we have this one,

00:41:46.470 --> 00:41:48.720
but even in the entire
world, when you look at it,

00:41:48.720 --> 00:41:50.280
there are only just a few of these,

00:41:50.280 --> 00:41:51.954
as you can see here on this slide.

00:41:51.954 --> 00:41:54.900
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:58.760
Why was this discovered in
the Silla capital of Gyeongju?

00:41:58.760 --> 00:41:59.970
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:41:59.970 --> 00:42:01.770
There's no way to really explain it.

00:42:02.804 --> 00:42:05.160
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:42:05.160 --> 00:42:06.540
And that shows you how deep

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:08.582
the connection between
Central Asia and Silla is.

00:42:08.582 --> 00:42:13.137
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:42:13.137 --> 00:42:16.800
And so you see a lot of bowls
or dishes that take this shape

00:42:16.800 --> 00:42:18.619
in Silla and Gaya.

00:42:18.619 --> 00:42:22.952
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:42:25.320 --> 00:42:28.475
And they resemble the kinds of glasses,

00:42:28.475 --> 00:42:31.650
or the cups that were used by the Vikings,

00:42:31.650 --> 00:42:33.240
as you can see with the horns,

00:42:33.240 --> 00:42:35.888
and also the ones used by the Mongols.

00:42:35.888 --> 00:42:38.850
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:41.040
And if you go to the museum in Tehran,

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:43.883
very similar items and artifacts
you can easily discover.

00:42:43.883 --> 00:42:48.537
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:42:48.537 --> 00:42:51.060
And in Silla, you can see representations,

00:42:51.060 --> 00:42:51.930
as you can see here,

00:42:51.930 --> 00:42:54.800
of people of Central Asian origin.

00:42:54.800 --> 00:42:59.133
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:43:03.240 --> 00:43:05.640
So you can see that there
was frequent interaction

00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:08.220
and acceptance of
culture from Central Asia

00:43:08.220 --> 00:43:10.291
into ancient Korea.

00:43:10.291 --> 00:43:12.660
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:43:12.660 --> 00:43:15.625
And now let me talk briefly
about the Japanese islands.

00:43:15.625 --> 00:43:17.100
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:19.909
There's a period called
the Yayoi Period in Japan.

00:43:19.909 --> 00:43:24.242
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:43:25.890 --> 00:43:29.497
It's a culture that began
around 4th century BCE.

00:43:29.497 --> 00:43:33.830
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:43:36.660 --> 00:43:37.650
It's around this period

00:43:37.650 --> 00:43:40.470
that they began to develop agrarianism,

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:45.470
and also bronze culture,
and also state building.

00:43:46.036 --> 00:43:49.530
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:43:49.530 --> 00:43:50.711
So where did the people

00:43:50.711 --> 00:43:53.640
who live in the Japanese archipelago,

00:43:53.640 --> 00:43:55.200
where did they come from?

00:43:55.200 --> 00:43:56.033
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:43:56.033 --> 00:43:58.137
Did they fly over to the islands?

00:43:58.137 --> 00:43:58.970
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:43:58.970 --> 00:44:00.131
Obviously not.

00:44:00.131 --> 00:44:01.140
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:02.967
They had to have taken ships over.

00:44:02.967 --> 00:44:07.300
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:44:13.950 --> 00:44:17.280
So there's not a lot of material artifact

00:44:17.280 --> 00:44:18.330
from the Jomon period,

00:44:18.330 --> 00:44:21.990
but we have a lot of evidence of Koreans

00:44:21.990 --> 00:44:24.000
arriving on the shores
of the Japanese islands

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:26.298
in large numbers during this period.

00:44:26.298 --> 00:44:27.570
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:44:27.570 --> 00:44:28.653
And this is a fact.

00:44:29.558 --> 00:44:33.891
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:44:37.761 --> 00:44:40.410
For the 4th century CE onwards,

00:44:40.410 --> 00:44:43.691
Japan begins to develop its own states.

00:44:43.691 --> 00:44:48.024
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:44:53.640 --> 00:44:55.770
And so unlike a lot of
other Korean scholars,

00:44:55.770 --> 00:44:57.660
he believes that from the
fourth century onwards,

00:44:57.660 --> 00:44:59.010
that the Japanese had developed

00:44:59.010 --> 00:45:01.376
very strong sense of
civilization and culture.

00:45:01.376 --> 00:45:04.800
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:45:04.800 --> 00:45:06.990
And I've explained all this very clearly

00:45:06.990 --> 00:45:08.500
in my publications already.

00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:09.600
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:45:09.600 --> 00:45:11.910
And last but not least,

00:45:11.910 --> 00:45:14.670
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:45:14.670 --> 00:45:16.923
in terms of Korea's relation with Japan,

00:45:18.028 --> 00:45:22.361
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:45:24.150 --> 00:45:27.637
the 70 year war with China,
Koguryo actually loses.

00:45:27.637 --> 00:45:29.160
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:45:29.160 --> 00:45:32.470
And Baekje will also collapse as well.

00:45:32.470 --> 00:45:35.400
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:45:35.400 --> 00:45:37.680
And a lot of immigrants from Baekje

00:45:37.680 --> 00:45:40.246
will cross the strait
into the Japanese islands.

00:45:40.246 --> 00:45:43.410
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:48.410
And immigrants from Koguryo
will also enter Japan as well.

00:45:49.479 --> 00:45:53.812
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:45:57.870 --> 00:46:01.140
And all of these people will
come together as a community

00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:02.310
and establish a new state,

00:46:02.310 --> 00:46:04.411
and that is the current Japanese state.

00:46:04.411 --> 00:46:08.744
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:46:09.810 --> 00:46:11.190
And this is my personal opinion,

00:46:11.190 --> 00:46:12.330
but he believes that this is

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:16.854
the first unified polity,
or state, in Japan.

00:46:16.854 --> 00:46:20.370
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:46:20.370 --> 00:46:24.006
The word nihon, or iribon, means

00:46:24.006 --> 00:46:27.124
"the place where the sun rises."

00:46:27.124 --> 00:46:31.817
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:46:31.817 --> 00:46:34.230
And I'll show you the
similarities, or the connections,

00:46:34.230 --> 00:46:36.365
between Korea and Japan.

00:46:36.365 --> 00:46:40.698
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:46:44.490 --> 00:46:46.740
So what you see here is a jade ornament,

00:46:46.740 --> 00:46:50.158
and a sword, and a
bronze mirror from Japan.

00:46:50.158 --> 00:46:54.491
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:47:04.800 --> 00:47:08.172
There was an effort by the Japanese

00:47:08.172 --> 00:47:12.210
at the end of the Second World
War to evade the Americans

00:47:12.210 --> 00:47:14.310
who were looking for
the treasures of Japan,

00:47:14.310 --> 00:47:16.320
the three items that you
see here on this slide,

00:47:16.320 --> 00:47:18.633
and the Japanese worked very diligently

00:47:18.633 --> 00:47:20.910
to try to keep these out of the reaches

00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:23.279
of the U.S. forces at the time.

00:47:23.279 --> 00:47:27.612
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:47:29.326 --> 00:47:32.280
And so I believe that the
Japanese, really, on the one hand,

00:47:32.280 --> 00:47:33.810
are scary,

00:47:33.810 --> 00:47:36.360
but also that they have a lot of focus

00:47:36.360 --> 00:47:38.099
to be able to pull
something like that off.

00:47:38.099 --> 00:47:42.360
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:47:42.360 --> 00:47:44.610
The shoes that you see on the left

00:47:44.610 --> 00:47:46.500
are bronze shoes from Japan.

00:47:46.500 --> 00:47:47.880
The one that you see on the right there

00:47:47.880 --> 00:47:48.981
is from ancient Korea.

00:47:48.981 --> 00:47:49.890
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:47:49.890 --> 00:47:52.210
And these were found in various locations.

00:47:52.210 --> 00:47:57.210
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:47:57.210 --> 00:48:00.420
And as you can see on the
left, bronze hats from Japan,

00:48:00.420 --> 00:48:02.556
on the right, bronze hats from Korea.

00:48:02.556 --> 00:48:05.430
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:48:05.430 --> 00:48:07.920
And you can see that the tail
ornament on the hats as well,

00:48:07.920 --> 00:48:10.479
there's a remarkable
resemblance between the two.

00:48:10.479 --> 00:48:14.812
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:48:17.670 --> 00:48:19.140
And you can see that the Gaya people,

00:48:19.140 --> 00:48:22.560
who were depicted on the map
right there in the orange,

00:48:22.560 --> 00:48:23.880
in the Korean Peninsula,

00:48:23.880 --> 00:48:26.280
they traveled over into
the Japanese islands,

00:48:26.280 --> 00:48:27.600
and they left behind artifacts

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:29.820
in all the areas that
are marked with the dots

00:48:29.820 --> 00:48:30.903
on the Japanese map.

00:48:31.842 --> 00:48:36.175
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:48:48.300 --> 00:48:51.750
Japan is certainly, no doubt,
a very powerful country,

00:48:51.750 --> 00:48:53.550
but we, we should remember

00:48:53.550 --> 00:48:56.700
that the formation of
civilization in Japan

00:48:56.700 --> 00:48:59.310
was a consequence of all of these Koreans

00:48:59.310 --> 00:49:01.440
crossing the strait into
the Japanese islands

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:02.439
during this period.

00:49:02.439 --> 00:49:04.140
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:49:04.140 --> 00:49:05.970
And this is the helmet for a horse

00:49:05.970 --> 00:49:06.856
that was discovered in ancient Japan.

00:49:06.856 --> 00:49:09.240
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:49:09.240 --> 00:49:11.370
And this is the helmet for a horse,

00:49:11.370 --> 00:49:12.450
oh, we skipped a slide,

00:49:12.450 --> 00:49:14.130
but the other one was from Japan,

00:49:14.130 --> 00:49:15.166
so you can see the similarities.

00:49:15.166 --> 00:49:19.230
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:49:19.230 --> 00:49:20.550
Bronze crown from Korea,

00:49:20.550 --> 00:49:22.418
bronze crown from Japan.

00:49:22.418 --> 00:49:23.368
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:49:23.368 --> 00:49:24.548
And this is important.

00:49:24.548 --> 00:49:29.250
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:49:29.250 --> 00:49:30.990
So this is treasure number one in Japan,

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:35.280
this is a seated Buddha,
or Bodhisattva figurine,

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:39.887
a wooden one that is
currently housed in Japan.

00:49:39.887 --> 00:49:42.120
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:49:42.120 --> 00:49:44.992
And this is the most
prized treasure of Japan.

00:49:44.992 --> 00:49:48.810
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:49:48.810 --> 00:49:51.780
And Carli Aspers once made mention

00:49:51.780 --> 00:49:54.240
of the aesthetic qualities of this item.

00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:56.880
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:49:56.880 --> 00:49:58.290
And I've crossed over to Japan

00:49:58.290 --> 00:50:00.690
to actually go look at this statue

00:50:00.690 --> 00:50:02.570
on more than 50 occasions.

00:50:02.570 --> 00:50:03.990
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:50:03.990 --> 00:50:07.046
But let's look at the image on the left.

00:50:07.046 --> 00:50:08.430
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:50:08.430 --> 00:50:10.442
This is a very important fact.

00:50:10.442 --> 00:50:12.120
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:13.906
I'll skip it though. (laughs)

00:50:13.906 --> 00:50:15.636
(students laughing)

00:50:15.636 --> 00:50:18.060
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:50:18.060 --> 00:50:19.680
Now let me talk about Manchuria.

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:20.579
- Yes, Manchuria.

00:50:20.579 --> 00:50:24.912
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:50:29.700 --> 00:50:33.930
- So, Korea has been heavily influenced

00:50:33.930 --> 00:50:38.930
by the cultures that entered
through the nomadic immigrants

00:50:39.270 --> 00:50:42.378
from Manchuria and other
parts of Central Asia.

00:50:42.378 --> 00:50:45.510
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:50:45.510 --> 00:50:49.343
And obviously forged a tight
bond with these people.

00:50:49.343 --> 00:50:51.990
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:50:51.990 --> 00:50:56.990
And there's an inevitable
geopolitical formation

00:50:57.060 --> 00:51:01.386
that existed in Northeast
Asia as a consequence.

00:51:01.386 --> 00:51:04.410
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:51:04.410 --> 00:51:09.348
There is the folks of the
steppes in the northwest.

00:51:09.348 --> 00:51:13.636
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:51:13.636 --> 00:51:14.469
And on the other hand,

00:51:14.469 --> 00:51:16.660
there is the great civilizations of China.

00:51:16.660 --> 00:51:18.960
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:51:18.960 --> 00:51:20.400
And these two civilizations

00:51:20.400 --> 00:51:23.130
have constantly been
fighting with each other

00:51:23.130 --> 00:51:24.592
and in conflict with each other.

00:51:24.592 --> 00:51:26.670
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:51:26.670 --> 00:51:29.451
But in the east, there was a
separate eastern civilization.

00:51:29.451 --> 00:51:32.190
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:51:32.190 --> 00:51:34.440
And even geopolitically speaking,

00:51:34.440 --> 00:51:36.848
this is a separate cultural sphere.

00:51:36.848 --> 00:51:41.181
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:51:44.280 --> 00:51:45.450
So, naturally,

00:51:45.450 --> 00:51:49.860
when the Chinese
civilizations became powerful,

00:51:49.860 --> 00:51:52.680
a bond, or a connection, or alliance

00:51:52.680 --> 00:51:55.290
was naturally formed
between the civilization

00:51:55.290 --> 00:51:56.550
up in the northern steppes,

00:51:56.550 --> 00:51:58.800
and the civilizations out in the east,

00:51:58.800 --> 00:52:00.836
which of course encompasses Korea.

00:52:00.836 --> 00:52:02.130
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:52:02.130 --> 00:52:04.697
And this is a very, very important fact.

00:52:04.697 --> 00:52:09.673
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:52:09.673 --> 00:52:10.506
So in Manchuria, there were
the Senbi, and also the Kitans.

00:52:14.242 --> 00:52:18.575
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:52:24.336 --> 00:52:25.410
(Professor Ahn chuckling)

00:52:25.410 --> 00:52:27.030
In China, it's hard,

00:52:27.030 --> 00:52:28.950
it's not frequently admitted,

00:52:28.950 --> 00:52:33.630
but Chinese history was
deeply and heavily shaped

00:52:33.630 --> 00:52:38.630
by the conquests and influences
of these northern people,

00:52:38.670 --> 00:52:39.679
the Senbi and the Kitans.

00:52:39.679 --> 00:52:43.470
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:52:43.470 --> 00:52:45.780
But the Senbis and the Kitans

00:52:45.780 --> 00:52:49.296
lived in the northeast in Manchuria.

00:52:49.296 --> 00:52:53.190
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:52:53.190 --> 00:52:55.954
According to historical record
from the mid-6th century,

00:52:55.954 --> 00:53:00.287
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:53:02.460 --> 00:53:03.870
there is a record that says

00:53:03.870 --> 00:53:05.790
that the horses of the
Senbi, and the Kitan,

00:53:05.790 --> 00:53:08.801
and the Koguryo, are
the same kind of horse.

00:53:08.801 --> 00:53:10.890
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:53:10.890 --> 00:53:13.645
And I think this I implies
something very, very important.

00:53:13.645 --> 00:53:15.810
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:53:15.810 --> 00:53:17.930
There's another important
fact about eastern Manchuria.

00:53:17.930 --> 00:53:19.530
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:21.908
There, the Tungusic people used to live.

00:53:21.908 --> 00:53:24.990
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:53:24.990 --> 00:53:27.030
The countries or the kingdoms established

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:30.990
by the Tungusic people
are the jin, and the?

00:53:30.990 --> 00:53:32.790
- Chen.
- Right, those were Chen.

00:53:32.790 --> 00:53:33.778
Yeah.

00:53:33.778 --> 00:53:38.697
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:53:38.697 --> 00:53:40.350
And so we need to take another second look

00:53:40.350 --> 00:53:41.965
at East Asian history.

00:53:41.965 --> 00:53:44.130
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:53:44.130 --> 00:53:46.653
So let's now take a look at China.

00:53:47.633 --> 00:53:50.130
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:53:50.130 --> 00:53:53.667
I really believe that Chinese
civilization is great.

00:53:53.667 --> 00:53:58.000
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:53:59.070 --> 00:54:00.750
Because it's located in the center,

00:54:00.750 --> 00:54:03.870
all the different cultural
and material influences

00:54:03.870 --> 00:54:06.193
from the peripheries entered into China.

00:54:06.193 --> 00:54:09.720
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:54:09.720 --> 00:54:12.540
A lot of people think
that the primary agent

00:54:12.540 --> 00:54:15.325
of Chinese history are the Han people.

00:54:15.325 --> 00:54:19.658
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:54:24.930 --> 00:54:28.770
The people who are really
responsible for the rise and fall

00:54:28.770 --> 00:54:30.540
in the development of Chinese civilization

00:54:30.540 --> 00:54:31.890
are these people who are located

00:54:31.890 --> 00:54:34.410
on the outskirts of the center,

00:54:34.410 --> 00:54:36.990
and through conquest and influence,

00:54:36.990 --> 00:54:40.780
they're going to help this
developmental process.

00:54:40.780 --> 00:54:42.330
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:54:42.330 --> 00:54:44.864
So, I proffer my own theory.

00:54:44.864 --> 00:54:49.197
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:54:52.082 --> 00:54:57.082
So China is a social,
cultural, and? (chuckles)

00:54:58.320 --> 00:54:59.940
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:54:59.940 --> 00:55:03.257
(Professor Youn and
Ahn speaking in Korean)

00:55:03.257 --> 00:55:06.928
And geographical entity,
and not actually a country.

00:55:06.928 --> 00:55:11.868
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:55:11.868 --> 00:55:15.960
The first time that China was
used as a name for a country

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:17.060
was through Soong Won.

00:55:17.929 --> 00:55:22.680
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:55:22.680 --> 00:55:25.350
China is great, but we
should always remember

00:55:25.350 --> 00:55:28.140
that it is a product of
the efforts of many people,

00:55:28.140 --> 00:55:29.462
and not just one.

00:55:29.462 --> 00:55:33.795
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:55:44.490 --> 00:55:46.890
And that's why a new geo-political order

00:55:46.890 --> 00:55:47.970
should be established

00:55:47.970 --> 00:55:50.790
through the collaboration
of the Han people,

00:55:50.790 --> 00:55:52.170
the mongoloids in the east,

00:55:52.170 --> 00:55:54.633
and the Turks in the west.

00:55:54.633 --> 00:55:56.910
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:55:56.910 --> 00:55:59.932
Now we're headed to the
epilogue of my talk.

00:55:59.932 --> 00:56:03.900
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:56:03.900 --> 00:56:07.859
Korea is a very unique,
longstanding historical community,

00:56:07.859 --> 00:56:11.850
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:56:11.850 --> 00:56:13.710
As I mentioned on Wednesday,

00:56:13.710 --> 00:56:16.620
this is a culture, or a community,

00:56:16.620 --> 00:56:18.570
an unbroken one that has continued

00:56:18.570 --> 00:56:20.981
from time memorial to the present.

00:56:20.981 --> 00:56:25.314
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:56:26.670 --> 00:56:29.220
The people who form
the core of Korea today

00:56:29.220 --> 00:56:30.690
aren't from the Korean Peninsula,

00:56:30.690 --> 00:56:32.070
but from southern Manchuria

00:56:32.070 --> 00:56:34.581
and the regions just north
of the Korean Peninsula.

00:56:34.581 --> 00:56:38.550
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:56:38.550 --> 00:56:42.630
And I've shown this very thoroughly

00:56:42.630 --> 00:56:44.861
in my first lecture on Wednesday.

00:56:44.861 --> 00:56:49.194
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:56:50.190 --> 00:56:52.170
Oh, for those of you who weren't able

00:56:52.170 --> 00:56:53.370
to come to the talk on Wednesday,

00:56:53.370 --> 00:56:58.034
please do ask your friends
about what I talked about.

00:56:58.034 --> 00:57:01.380
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:57:01.380 --> 00:57:02.640
As I mentioned on Wednesday,

00:57:02.640 --> 00:57:05.040
I see the ocean surrounding Korea

00:57:05.040 --> 00:57:06.754
as a kind of Mediterranean.

00:57:06.754 --> 00:57:11.087
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:57:12.750 --> 00:57:17.190
Korea is an organic
maritime cultural sphere

00:57:17.190 --> 00:57:19.290
that consists not only of the territories

00:57:19.290 --> 00:57:21.300
of the Korean Peninsula, the land mass,

00:57:21.300 --> 00:57:22.865
but also the surrounding oceans.

00:57:22.865 --> 00:57:27.198
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:57:28.262 --> 00:57:29.970
Oh, and he also believes that China

00:57:29.970 --> 00:57:32.523
is aspiring to be a
maritime culture as well.

00:57:32.523 --> 00:57:35.130
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:57:35.130 --> 00:57:38.550
Because China was always
a continental entity,

00:57:38.550 --> 00:57:41.229
and not necessarily a maritime one.

00:57:41.229 --> 00:57:45.562
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:57:54.360 --> 00:57:57.270
And that's explains why there
are the frequent clashes

00:57:57.270 --> 00:57:58.860
between the Chinese forces today

00:57:58.860 --> 00:58:02.306
with the Japanese and the
Americans in the surrounding seas.

00:58:02.306 --> 00:58:06.798
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:58:06.798 --> 00:58:09.060
And I've talked about all of this

00:58:09.060 --> 00:58:11.793
in a very neat and concise way
in a book that I published.

00:58:11.793 --> 00:58:16.126
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:58:18.420 --> 00:58:23.420
Oh, he wants others to take
into account his research

00:58:23.550 --> 00:58:25.054
when they speak of the subject.

00:58:25.054 --> 00:58:28.080
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:58:28.080 --> 00:58:30.694
I'm a Korean, so I'm deeply
passionate about Korea.

00:58:30.694 --> 00:58:33.029
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:58:33.029 --> 00:58:35.036
Korea has a very unique ecosystem.

00:58:35.036 --> 00:58:39.271
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:58:39.271 --> 00:58:42.181
Has a very meaningful culture and history.

00:58:42.181 --> 00:58:43.140
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:58:43.140 --> 00:58:44.566
And this is for real.

00:58:44.566 --> 00:58:45.399
(Professors Youn and Ahn laughing)

00:58:45.399 --> 00:58:46.232
It's the truth.

00:58:46.232 --> 00:58:47.065
- Okay.

00:58:47.065 --> 00:58:51.398
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:58:55.860 --> 00:58:58.560
- So Korea has a different
thought and culture

00:58:58.560 --> 00:59:02.160
from the cultures of China, the northeast,

00:59:02.160 --> 00:59:03.811
and also from Japan as well.

00:59:03.811 --> 00:59:08.100
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:59:08.100 --> 00:59:12.900
But even the Koreans
themselves have lost track of

00:59:12.900 --> 00:59:14.585
and forgotten this culture.

00:59:14.585 --> 00:59:18.918
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:59:20.640 --> 00:59:24.570
And he wants Koreans to
once again recall and know

00:59:24.570 --> 00:59:28.180
this very magnificent and great culture

00:59:28.180 --> 00:59:29.263
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:59:29.263 --> 00:59:31.349
And I want you all to know it as well.

00:59:31.349 --> 00:59:35.682
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:59:39.120 --> 00:59:43.500
And he wishes for Korean
history, culture, and thought

00:59:43.500 --> 00:59:47.070
to be used as a very
useful and effective tool

00:59:47.070 --> 00:59:50.134
in solving a lot of the
problems that we face today.

00:59:50.134 --> 00:59:52.309
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:59:52.309 --> 00:59:55.057
And that is why I stand
here before you today.

00:59:55.057 --> 00:59:55.890
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

00:59:55.890 --> 00:59:56.723
Thank you all very much.

00:59:56.723 --> 00:59:59.890
(students applauding)

01:00:01.320 --> 01:00:02.580
- Thank you so much.

01:00:02.580 --> 01:00:05.190
You are very informative, enlightening,

01:00:05.190 --> 01:00:06.780
and a very constructive talk,

01:00:06.780 --> 01:00:09.600
and we learned so much from your talk,

01:00:09.600 --> 01:00:10.433
two talks.

01:00:10.433 --> 01:00:11.266
(Professor Ahn laughing)

01:00:11.266 --> 01:00:14.733
Now it's time to get your
questions from the audience.

01:00:16.680 --> 01:00:19.200
Those of you who have
written your questions

01:00:19.200 --> 01:00:22.290
on the note cards,

01:00:22.290 --> 01:00:24.513
you can just forward them to me.

01:00:40.770 --> 01:00:44.943
Why did you use Manchuria to describe,

01:00:49.860 --> 01:00:50.974
oh, the handwriting, you guys.

01:00:50.974 --> 01:00:52.347
(Students and professor Ahn laughing)

01:00:52.347 --> 01:00:55.200
(Professor Youn coughing)

01:00:55.200 --> 01:00:56.550
- [Student] Manchuria
to describe the location

01:00:56.550 --> 01:00:58.300
of the Koguryo tombs?

01:01:00.390 --> 01:01:02.490
Okay, apparently I'm reading it.

01:01:02.490 --> 01:01:04.680
Why did you use Manchuria to describe

01:01:04.680 --> 01:01:07.846
the location of the Koguryo tombs?

01:01:07.846 --> 01:01:12.096
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:01:18.244 --> 01:01:22.560
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:01:22.560 --> 01:01:24.600
Because the Koguryo capital

01:01:24.600 --> 01:01:28.563
was located in what is today
Manchuria, not in Korea.

01:01:30.751 --> 01:01:34.500
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:01:34.500 --> 01:01:39.500
Yeah, and the origins of the
Korean people, he believes,

01:01:39.690 --> 01:01:41.760
is the areas of southern Manchuria

01:01:41.760 --> 01:01:46.723
and the areas just north of
the Korean Peninsula. (laughs)

01:01:50.580 --> 01:01:52.417
- [Student] Okay, the next one says,

01:01:52.417 --> 01:01:54.027
"Have you been to Pyongyang?"

01:01:55.051 --> 01:01:57.390
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:01:57.390 --> 01:01:58.740
- Two times.

01:01:58.740 --> 01:02:00.140
- He's been there two times.

01:02:03.810 --> 01:02:06.240
- No, no, one time's
Pyongyang, and one time's Keso.

01:02:06.240 --> 01:02:09.027
- Oh, so, he's been to Pyongyang once,

01:02:09.027 --> 01:02:12.414
and the other trip was to
the old Kora capital, Keso.

01:02:12.414 --> 01:02:13.836
- And the Gujyeongson. Three times.

01:02:13.836 --> 01:02:15.093
- And Gujyeonson, yeah.

01:02:16.590 --> 01:02:18.607
- [Student] And then
the third one here says,

01:02:18.607 --> 01:02:21.930
"How can we identify the
ambassadors in the mural,"

01:02:21.930 --> 01:02:24.277
oh, I guess the two mural,

01:02:24.277 --> 01:02:27.610
"as Koguryo and not Baekje?"

01:02:28.609 --> 01:02:32.859
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:02:36.870 --> 01:02:40.110
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:02:40.110 --> 01:02:42.180
- There is some dispute and debate

01:02:42.180 --> 01:02:47.180
about where the envoys are
from in the mural in Samarkand.

01:02:48.656 --> 01:02:53.656
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:02:53.790 --> 01:02:57.270
But based on circumstantial
evidence from that period,

01:02:57.270 --> 01:03:00.563
it's clear that the envoys
must be envoys from Koguryo.

01:03:00.563 --> 01:03:02.160
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:03:02.160 --> 01:03:03.900
The hat, for example.

01:03:03.900 --> 01:03:05.610
- And the back too.

01:03:05.610 --> 01:03:06.679
- And the belt.

01:03:06.679 --> 01:03:07.530
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:03:07.530 --> 01:03:08.363
And the sword.

01:03:09.438 --> 01:03:10.271
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:03:10.271 --> 01:03:11.863
And their shoes.

01:03:11.863 --> 01:03:15.930
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:03:15.930 --> 01:03:17.970
And so scholars use that as evidence

01:03:17.970 --> 01:03:20.700
to conclude that those envoys

01:03:20.700 --> 01:03:22.850
are envoys from Koguryo,
and not elsewhere.

01:03:25.170 --> 01:03:28.076
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:03:28.076 --> 01:03:29.808
(student speaking Korean)

01:03:29.808 --> 01:03:31.463
(students and professors
Youn and Ahn laughing)

01:03:31.463 --> 01:03:35.395
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:03:35.395 --> 01:03:38.844
Please come to Samarkand, I'll
show you the mural. (laughs)

01:03:38.844 --> 01:03:39.960
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:03:39.960 --> 01:03:41.880
It's a beautiful place, Samarkand.

01:03:41.880 --> 01:03:43.085
I agree.

01:03:43.085 --> 01:03:44.370
- [Assistant] Okay.

01:03:44.370 --> 01:03:45.247
- [Student] The next one says,

01:03:45.247 --> 01:03:47.430
"Some people might say history

01:03:47.430 --> 01:03:48.840
is something that happened in the past,

01:03:48.840 --> 01:03:51.270
why should we pay
attention to past things?

01:03:51.270 --> 01:03:52.650
We are living in the present.

01:03:52.650 --> 01:03:54.570
How should we explain the significance

01:03:54.570 --> 01:03:57.017
of studying history to these people?"

01:03:57.017 --> 01:04:01.267
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:04:07.079 --> 01:04:10.620
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:04:10.620 --> 01:04:14.670
He believes that history
is the study of the future.

01:04:14.670 --> 01:04:15.759
- Yes.

01:04:15.759 --> 01:04:20.092
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:04:21.480 --> 01:04:23.130
- All the things that are in existence,

01:04:23.130 --> 01:04:24.660
they exist not for the past,

01:04:24.660 --> 01:04:26.460
not for the present necessarily,

01:04:26.460 --> 01:04:28.073
but for the future.

01:04:28.073 --> 01:04:32.406
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:04:35.010 --> 01:04:37.083
So history is.

01:04:37.083 --> 01:04:38.700
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:04:38.700 --> 01:04:39.533
Yeah.

01:04:39.533 --> 01:04:41.700
So history is a guide that shows you

01:04:41.700 --> 01:04:44.118
how we're all going to
be living in the future.

01:04:44.118 --> 01:04:45.540
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:04:45.540 --> 01:04:49.053
So the history as a guide is,

01:04:51.240 --> 01:04:52.073
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:04:52.073 --> 01:04:52.906
a standard.

01:04:52.906 --> 01:04:55.080
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:04:55.080 --> 01:04:56.400
It's a standard or a paradigm,

01:04:56.400 --> 01:05:00.393
and not necessarily a prediction
or kind of fortune telling.

01:05:04.260 --> 01:05:05.093
- [Student] This says,

01:05:05.093 --> 01:05:07.350
"If the castle was used
to protect the commoners,

01:05:07.350 --> 01:05:11.424
what did the elites at that
time use for extra protection?"

01:05:11.424 --> 01:05:15.674
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:05:19.853 --> 01:05:24.186
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:05:39.954 --> 01:05:41.850
So the Koguryo castle actually consists

01:05:41.850 --> 01:05:43.110
of two separate structures.

01:05:43.110 --> 01:05:44.880
There's an inner structure
where, of course,

01:05:44.880 --> 01:05:46.530
it was reserved for the elite,

01:05:46.530 --> 01:05:47.940
but at times of war,

01:05:47.940 --> 01:05:50.580
the people who would be living
outside of the castle walls

01:05:50.580 --> 01:05:52.770
would bring all of their
valuables into the wall,

01:05:52.770 --> 01:05:55.290
and they would occupy the
areas inside the city,

01:05:55.290 --> 01:05:56.393
where they will be protected.

01:05:56.393 --> 01:06:00.880
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:06:00.880 --> 01:06:02.670
So he believes that the history of Korea

01:06:02.670 --> 01:06:06.030
is not the history of the few elites,

01:06:06.030 --> 01:06:08.822
but rather the history of its people.

01:06:08.822 --> 01:06:13.155
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:06:15.780 --> 01:06:16.923
They're all family,

01:06:18.300 --> 01:06:20.250
they're not people who
use a different language,

01:06:20.250 --> 01:06:21.450
or had different ideas,

01:06:21.450 --> 01:06:23.053
but, really, a family.

01:06:23.053 --> 01:06:25.710
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:06:25.710 --> 01:06:27.450
And that's the difference between Korea

01:06:27.450 --> 01:06:29.490
and the other surrounding countries.

01:06:29.490 --> 01:06:30.783
- Okay. Now?

01:06:32.250 --> 01:06:34.590
- [Student] What stereotype
or misunderstanding of Korea

01:06:34.590 --> 01:06:36.243
do you think is most damaging?

01:06:37.124 --> 01:06:41.374
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:06:43.005 --> 01:06:47.338
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:06:49.958 --> 01:06:52.710
- So, he believes that
the greatest stereotype,

01:06:52.710 --> 01:06:53.820
and the worst,

01:06:53.820 --> 01:06:58.820
is the one that depicts Koreans
as people of Han, or sorrow,

01:06:59.130 --> 01:07:00.360
or melancholy,

01:07:00.360 --> 01:07:02.700
and also people who
serve the great, right,

01:07:02.700 --> 01:07:04.740
who practice, who have always practiced,

01:07:04.740 --> 01:07:06.363
a kind of toadyism to China.

01:07:14.137 --> 01:07:17.100
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:07:17.100 --> 01:07:19.500
Oh, he thinks his lecture's a success,

01:07:19.500 --> 01:07:21.210
given the large number of questions

01:07:21.210 --> 01:07:22.050
that he's getting. (laughs)

01:07:22.050 --> 01:07:22.883
I agree.

01:07:23.760 --> 01:07:25.050
- [Student] Did the Jomon culture

01:07:25.050 --> 01:07:28.080
have any cultural influence
on the Korean Peninsula

01:07:28.080 --> 01:07:30.483
during the period of migration?

01:07:32.262 --> 01:07:36.726
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:07:36.726 --> 01:07:41.059
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:07:45.659 --> 01:07:49.860
So, the Jomon culture, or
civilization, is one of the oldest

01:07:49.860 --> 01:07:52.783
and most important ancient civilizations.

01:07:52.783 --> 01:07:56.712
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:07:56.712 --> 01:07:58.402
Busan. The city of Busan that
most of you probably know.

01:07:58.402 --> 01:07:59.926
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:07:59.926 --> 01:08:01.034
And Ulsan, another city in Korea.

01:08:01.034 --> 01:08:05.451
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:08:08.100 --> 01:08:10.920
And cultural artifacts of the Jomon Period

01:08:10.920 --> 01:08:13.170
were discovered in these Korean cities,

01:08:13.170 --> 01:08:14.217
located in the south east.

01:08:14.217 --> 01:08:18.540
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:08:18.540 --> 01:08:22.440
And based on new genetic evidence,

01:08:22.440 --> 01:08:25.320
it's clear that the
people of the Jomon Period

01:08:25.320 --> 01:08:26.850
crossed over and intermixed

01:08:26.850 --> 01:08:28.386
with the people of the Korean Peninsula.

01:08:28.386 --> 01:08:31.680
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:08:31.680 --> 01:08:32.800
But Koreans don't acknowledge this,

01:08:32.800 --> 01:08:34.110
(Professor Youn laughing)

01:08:34.110 --> 01:08:37.745
and don't really know
that this is the fact.

01:08:37.745 --> 01:08:38.578
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:08:38.578 --> 01:08:39.900
And this is a big problem.

01:08:39.900 --> 01:08:40.733
- Okay.

01:08:42.270 --> 01:08:44.280
- [Student] What outside
cultural influences

01:08:44.280 --> 01:08:47.361
do you see permeating
Korean society today?

01:08:47.361 --> 01:08:51.611
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:08:54.841 --> 01:08:55.691
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:08:55.691 --> 01:08:58.203
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:08:58.203 --> 01:08:59.940
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:08:59.940 --> 01:09:01.383
- This is difficult to say,

01:09:03.360 --> 01:09:05.492
but the Haiyu that you all like,

01:09:05.492 --> 01:09:07.530
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:09:07.530 --> 01:09:10.105
I don't see that as a
positive development.

01:09:10.105 --> 01:09:13.350
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:09:13.350 --> 01:09:18.350
Because Haiyu was heavily
influenced by Western culture.

01:09:18.612 --> 01:09:22.470
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:09:22.470 --> 01:09:26.000
But the ancient Haiyu, very great.

01:09:26.000 --> 01:09:30.333
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:09:35.520 --> 01:09:38.580
So he really wants the BTS's of the world

01:09:38.580 --> 01:09:40.380
to take into account

01:09:40.380 --> 01:09:43.110
the aesthetic and cultural
values of the past,

01:09:43.110 --> 01:09:44.430
the Haiyu of the past,

01:09:44.430 --> 01:09:47.380
to create new Korean culture
of the present and the future.

01:09:48.720 --> 01:09:50.700
- [Student] Is it possible
to have unification

01:09:50.700 --> 01:09:51.960
of the two Koreas?

01:09:51.960 --> 01:09:54.091
How soon, in your view?

01:09:54.091 --> 01:09:58.341
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:10:01.228 --> 01:10:03.540
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:10:03.540 --> 01:10:06.016
- He would like to know
who asked the question.

01:10:06.016 --> 01:10:07.474
(attendant speaking distantly)

01:10:07.474 --> 01:10:08.495
(student laughing)

01:10:08.495 --> 01:10:10.411
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:10:10.411 --> 01:10:13.010
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:10:13.010 --> 01:10:17.343
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:10:19.860 --> 01:10:21.570
So, it's a very difficult question,

01:10:21.570 --> 01:10:24.270
but he believes that unification
is absolutely necessary

01:10:24.270 --> 01:10:26.889
because we're one people.

01:10:26.889 --> 01:10:31.222
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:10:33.540 --> 01:10:34.800
He believes that people

01:10:34.800 --> 01:10:37.380
don't just have biological
or physical DNA,

01:10:37.380 --> 01:10:39.937
but also a cultural and
historical one as well.

01:10:39.937 --> 01:10:43.110
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:10:43.110 --> 01:10:45.780
And so he believes that
only through unification

01:10:45.780 --> 01:10:47.210
can we create --

01:10:49.093 --> 01:10:51.570
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:10:51.570 --> 01:10:54.154
But that's very difficult to achieve.

01:10:54.154 --> 01:10:55.290
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:10:55.290 --> 01:10:56.932
And that's why there are
so many difficulties.

01:10:56.932 --> 01:10:59.220
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:10:59.220 --> 01:11:04.220
I've made announcements about
this using my own thoughts.

01:11:04.363 --> 01:11:08.696
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:11:18.900 --> 01:11:22.500
So we, of course, all want unification,

01:11:22.500 --> 01:11:25.560
but for now, what we need to aspire for

01:11:25.560 --> 01:11:27.450
is peaceful coexistence,

01:11:27.450 --> 01:11:29.370
and creating the foundations

01:11:29.370 --> 01:11:32.190
that will allow us to eventually
achieve the highest goal,

01:11:32.190 --> 01:11:33.348
which is of course unification.

01:11:33.348 --> 01:11:37.681
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:11:44.700 --> 01:11:47.370
Because he's a Korean and
he really wants unification,

01:11:47.370 --> 01:11:49.438
he went to Borya in today's Russia.

01:11:49.438 --> 01:11:50.340
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:11:50.340 --> 01:11:51.660
- And he asked a shaman,

01:11:51.660 --> 01:11:55.471
and asked a shaman, "When is
Korea going to be unified?"

01:11:55.471 --> 01:11:56.790
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:11:56.790 --> 01:11:58.611
And his answer was?

01:11:58.611 --> 01:11:59.819
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:11:59.819 --> 01:12:01.020
(students laughing)

01:12:01.020 --> 01:12:02.440
It'll eventually happen.

01:12:02.440 --> 01:12:03.960
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:12:03.960 --> 01:12:05.523
Speechless.

01:12:05.523 --> 01:12:08.190
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:12:08.190 --> 01:12:10.928
The international relations, nobody can.

01:12:10.928 --> 01:12:14.553
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:12:14.553 --> 01:12:15.386
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:12:15.386 --> 01:12:17.989
Yeah, so there's a guy named
George Freeman, very famous.

01:12:17.989 --> 01:12:20.400
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:12:20.400 --> 01:12:22.068
And he wrote this book.

01:12:22.068 --> 01:12:25.812
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:12:25.812 --> 01:12:28.710
And 10 years after he published the book,

01:12:28.710 --> 01:12:30.420
more than half of what he predicted

01:12:30.420 --> 01:12:31.908
didn't actually come true.

01:12:31.908 --> 01:12:33.420
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:12:33.420 --> 01:12:36.119
So we can't really accurately
predict the future.

01:12:36.119 --> 01:12:39.750
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:12:39.750 --> 01:12:43.740
But history, as a larger framework

01:12:43.740 --> 01:12:47.118
for understanding the future,
can make some reliable.

01:12:47.118 --> 01:12:51.810
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:12:51.810 --> 01:12:54.063
Religion does fortune telling, but,

01:12:55.680 --> 01:12:57.304
history can make factual predictions.

01:12:57.304 --> 01:12:58.920
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:12:58.920 --> 01:13:00.030
And so, as a historian,

01:13:00.030 --> 01:13:02.583
I truly believe that
unification will happen.

01:13:03.600 --> 01:13:05.183
- [Assistant] One last question.

01:13:06.247 --> 01:13:08.040
- [Student] "What was it
like to live in Samarkand

01:13:08.040 --> 01:13:09.690
and teach at the university there?

01:13:09.690 --> 01:13:11.280
Did you face any challenges working

01:13:11.280 --> 01:13:12.630
with your colleagues at the department?

01:13:12.630 --> 01:13:13.710
Did you enjoy it?"

01:13:13.710 --> 01:13:16.208
The person who asked this
question is from there.

01:13:16.208 --> 01:13:20.541
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:13:23.970 --> 01:13:24.952
- Best.

01:13:24.952 --> 01:13:26.190
(students laughing)

01:13:26.190 --> 01:13:28.112
- [Professor Ahn] You all
got that right? (laughs)

01:13:28.112 --> 01:13:32.445
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:13:40.980 --> 01:13:43.980
- [Professor Ahn] He believes
that his most ideal community

01:13:43.980 --> 01:13:46.556
currently resides there in Samarkand.

01:13:46.556 --> 01:13:47.389
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:13:47.389 --> 01:13:49.350
(student speaking distantly)

01:13:49.350 --> 01:13:50.183
- [Assistant] Oh my,
you must be so educated.

01:13:50.183 --> 01:13:52.707
(students laughing)

01:13:52.707 --> 01:13:57.040
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:14:06.570 --> 01:14:08.100
- [Professor Ahn] So the
people in Uzbekistan,

01:14:08.100 --> 01:14:11.670
they don't just blindly
accept or negate civilization,

01:14:11.670 --> 01:14:14.043
they live as a family.

01:14:15.480 --> 01:14:17.370
It's a tight-knit community,

01:14:17.370 --> 01:14:18.900
and that's why he considers it

01:14:18.900 --> 01:14:21.738
to be the most ideal community on earth.

01:14:21.738 --> 01:14:26.250
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:14:26.250 --> 01:14:28.770
I didn't go to Samarkand to be a professor

01:14:28.770 --> 01:14:30.019
and have an income.

01:14:30.019 --> 01:14:34.352
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:14:37.140 --> 01:14:39.690
It's because he wanted to
see for himself, personally,

01:14:39.690 --> 01:14:42.398
this ideal community that
he was always yearning for.

01:14:42.398 --> 01:14:46.323
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:14:46.323 --> 01:14:50.010
And so he's hoping that
based on what he's witnessed

01:14:50.010 --> 01:14:52.710
and what he's learned from his time there,

01:14:52.710 --> 01:14:55.564
that others will see his views to be true.

01:14:55.564 --> 01:14:56.580
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:14:56.580 --> 01:14:58.233
He really likes Uzbekistan.

01:15:00.900 --> 01:15:02.010
- [Assistant] What are your last words

01:15:02.010 --> 01:15:04.239
for our Yale students?

01:15:04.239 --> 01:15:07.190
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:15:07.190 --> 01:15:11.523
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:15:15.390 --> 01:15:17.610
- [Professor Ahn] So
there was a big article

01:15:17.610 --> 01:15:20.010
about these lectures that
Professor Youn is giving

01:15:20.010 --> 01:15:21.464
in Korean newspapers.

01:15:21.464 --> 01:15:22.890
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:15:22.890 --> 01:15:24.989
And that was a mistake that I made.

01:15:24.989 --> 01:15:29.322
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:15:34.320 --> 01:15:35.760
I should have waited to hear

01:15:35.760 --> 01:15:38.040
all of these wonderful
comments and questions

01:15:38.040 --> 01:15:41.070
to share with the community
back at home in Korea,

01:15:41.070 --> 01:15:42.336
that would've been more ideal.

01:15:42.336 --> 01:15:43.770
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:15:43.770 --> 01:15:45.758
So I thank you all very, very much.

01:15:45.758 --> 01:15:46.785
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:15:46.785 --> 01:15:48.184
(students clapping)

01:15:48.184 --> 01:15:49.061
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:15:49.061 --> 01:15:51.101
(students laughing)

01:15:51.101 --> 01:15:55.434
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:15:58.426 --> 01:16:02.610
He's really thankful for all
of you having the patience,

01:16:02.610 --> 01:16:04.702
and to take all of what he's saying,

01:16:04.702 --> 01:16:06.210
soaking this so sincerely,

01:16:06.210 --> 01:16:08.210
and then asking all of these questions.

01:16:08.210 --> 01:16:12.690
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:16:12.690 --> 01:16:14.817
Why would America be so important?

01:16:14.817 --> 01:16:18.120
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:16:18.120 --> 01:16:19.290
It's because this is a place

01:16:19.290 --> 01:16:22.448
where everything and everyone is gathered.

01:16:22.448 --> 01:16:24.329
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:16:24.329 --> 01:16:25.162
(Professor Ahn speaking in Korean)

01:16:25.162 --> 01:16:25.995
- Okay.

01:16:25.995 --> 01:16:29.790
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:16:29.790 --> 01:16:32.820
- And he believes that he can
find the future of humanity

01:16:32.820 --> 01:16:34.595
here in the United States.

01:16:34.595 --> 01:16:37.230
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:16:37.230 --> 01:16:38.910
And the only place where that's possible

01:16:38.910 --> 01:16:39.784
is the place right here.

01:16:39.784 --> 01:16:44.100
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:16:44.100 --> 01:16:45.240
And this is the place

01:16:45.240 --> 01:16:46.253
where we're looking for
the future of humanity.

01:16:46.253 --> 01:16:47.398
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:16:47.398 --> 01:16:48.420
- Are you referring to Yale?

01:16:48.420 --> 01:16:49.675
- Yes, sir.
(many laughing)

01:16:49.675 --> 01:16:54.675
(Professor Youn speaking in Korean)

01:16:54.780 --> 01:16:58.470
- [Professor Ahn] And the agents
responsive for doing this,

01:16:58.470 --> 01:16:59.640
who will be doing this,

01:16:59.640 --> 01:17:02.061
are all of you here in this room.

01:17:02.061 --> 01:17:05.144
(everyone clapping)

01:17:07.560 --> 01:17:12.560
- Okay, finally we conclude
our two very important talks.

01:17:13.170 --> 01:17:14.003
- Okay.

01:17:14.003 --> 01:17:16.770
- And it was such an honor
to have you with us at Yale.

01:17:16.770 --> 01:17:18.930
And I hope, we hope,

01:17:18.930 --> 01:17:21.210
to have you back again in the near future.

01:17:21.210 --> 01:17:22.043
- Okay.

01:17:22.043 --> 01:17:22.996
- Thank you so much.
- Thanks.

01:17:22.996 --> 01:17:24.000
(students applauding)

01:17:24.000 --> 01:17:25.320
- Have a wonderful weekend.

01:17:25.320 --> 01:17:28.023
Thank you so much, you guys, for coming.

01:17:31.702 --> 01:17:34.285
(gentle music)

